# RandomSkunk.StructuredLogging

This project references the `RandomSkunk.StructuredLogging` NuGet package. It replaces
`Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions`'s `LogDebug`/`LogInformation`/`LogWarning`/etc.
extension methods, which force every structured property into the message template
(`logger.LogInformation("User {UserId} logged in", userId)`). This library lets the message be
worded however you want while structured properties are attached separately and explicitly.

When writing or editing log calls in this project, prefer the extension methods below over the
standard `ILogger.Log*` methods.

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using RandomSkunk.StructuredLogging; // brings the extension methods into scope
```

## The basic call

One extension method per `LogLevel` — `Trace`, `Debug`, `Information`, `Warning`, `Error`,
`Critical` — plus a level-as-argument `Write`. The message parameter accepts a normal
interpolated string:

```csharp
logger.Information($"User {userId} logged in");
logger.Write(level, $"User {userId} logged in");
```

Each level method (and `Write`) has 4 overloads for the leading `EventId`/`Exception`
arguments — both, either, or neither — always in this order: `logger`, `[eventId]`,
`[exception]`, `message`, ...:

```csharp
logger.Information(MyEventIds.UserLoggedIn, $"User {userId} logged in");   // EventId only
logger.Error(ex, $"Failed to process order {orderId}");                    // Exception only
logger.Error(MyEventIds.OrderFailed, ex, $"Failed to process order {orderId}"); // both
logger.Write(level, MyEventIds.OrderFailed, ex, $"Failed to process order {orderId}"); // Write takes level first
```

**Do not** wrap these calls in `if (logger.IsEnabled(LogLevel.X))` — the message parameter is a
custom `[InterpolatedStringHandler]`, not a plain `string`. Its constructor checks
`logger.IsEnabled(level)` first, and if the level is disabled the interpolation holes are never
evaluated (not appended, not formatted) — so `logger.Debug($"... {ExpensiveCall()}")` never calls
`ExpensiveCall()` when Debug is disabled. This is already handled; an extra `IsEnabled` guard is
redundant. A plain `string` (no interpolation) is also accepted and always treated as enabled.

## Attaching structured properties

There are three ways to attach properties to a call, chosen by what you know at the call site.

### 1. Statically-known properties (preferred, up to 6)

Pass one `(string Name, T Value)` tuple argument per property, after the message. These generic
overloads avoid boxing the values until/unless a log provider actually reads them (many simple
sinks never do), unlike the two options below which box eagerly:

```csharp
logger.Information($"Order processed", ("OrderId", orderId), ("Total", total));
logger.Warning(MyEventIds.SlowRequest, $"Slow request", ("Path", path), ("DurationMs", elapsedMs));
```

### 2. `<PropertyName>` format tags — capture a value that's *also* in the message

An interpolation hole's format can start with an "html-like" tag to append the value to the
message text *and* capture its raw (unformatted) value as a structured property of that name:

```csharp
logger.Information($"User {userId:<UserId>} logged in from {ip:<IpAddress>}");
// message text: "User 42 logged in from 10.0.0.1"
// structured properties: UserId = 42 (int), IpAddress = <IPAddress instance>

logger.Debug($"[{ts:<Timestamp>HH:mm:ss}] tick");
// message text uses "HH:mm:ss" to format ts; the Timestamp property holds the raw DateTime
```

An empty tag (`<>`) strips itself out without capturing — use it when a real format string
happens to start with `<`: `{value:<>therealformat}`.

### 3. Dynamic / more than 6 properties — `params (string Name, object? Value)[]`

```csharp
logger.Information($"Order processed", ("OrderId", orderId), ("Total", total), ("Currency", currency), ("Tax", tax), ("Discount", discount), ("Shipping", shipping), ("CouponCode", coupon));
```

Every generic-tuple call above (§1) is really calling one specific overload per arity; once you
need more than 6 properties or the set is built at runtime, use the `params` overload instead —
same call shape, boxed `object?` values.

### 4. Merging a pre-built collection with per-call properties

If you already have an `IReadOnlyCollection<KeyValuePair<string, object?>>` (e.g. a
`Dictionary<string, object?>` of ambient/scope properties), every shape above has a sibling
overload that takes it as a leading parameter (right after `logger`, before `eventId`/`exception`
if present). The trailing per-call properties parameter is then named `additionalLogProperty1..6`
or `additionalLogProperties`:

```csharp
logger.Warning(scopeProperties, MyEventIds.SlowRequest, $"Slow request to {path:<Route>}", ("DurationMs", elapsedMs));
```

Properties end up in this order: the collection's entries, then tag-captured properties, then the
`additionalLogProperty*`/`additionalLogProperties` arguments.

## Migrating from `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging`

```csharp
// Before:
logger.LogInformation("User {UserId} logged in from {IpAddress}", userId, ipAddress);

// After — properties still shown in the message text:
logger.Information($"User {userId:<UserId>} logged in from {ipAddress:<IpAddress>}");

// After — message worded freely, properties attached separately:
logger.Information($"User logged in", ("UserId", userId), ("IpAddress", ipAddress));
```

Don't mix the two libraries' extension methods in the same codebase/file — if the project has
adopted `RandomSkunk.StructuredLogging`, use its `Trace`/`Debug`/`Information`/`Warning`/`Error`/
`Critical`/`Write` methods instead of `LogTrace`/`LogDebug`/`LogInformation`/`LogWarning`/
`LogError`/`LogCritical`/`Log`.
